# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
plato-republic-1.md:1: argue/readings: S1 is read 2 ways: plato-republic-1.logic.r1 and plato-republic-1.logic.r1.3 have "fares_well" and "fares_well → no_principle"; plato-republic-1.logic.r1.2 has "fares_well"; they differ when only "if we unite the worst injustice with a deceitful regard to appearances, we shall fare to our mind with gods and men, in life and after death" is true, which the second allows
plato-republic-1.md:1: argue/answered: S1: answered no, which keeps plato-republic-1.logic.r1 and plato-republic-1.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "if we unite the worst injustice with a deceitful regard to appearances, we shall fare to our mind with gods and men, in life and after death" is true and "there is no principle on which we shall any longer choose justice rather than the worst injustice" is false. Is S1, "On what principle, then, shall we any longer choose justice rather than the worst injustice? when, if we only unite the latter with a deceitful regard to appearances, we shall fare to our mind both with gods and men, in life and after death, as the most numerous and the highest authorities tell us.", true then?
plato-republic-1.md:1: argue/readings: S4 is read 2 ways: plato-republic-1.logic.r1 has "blames_injustice → lacks_power"; plato-republic-1.logic.r1.2 and plato-republic-1.logic.r1.3 have nothing from it; they differ when only "he blames injustice" is true, which the second allows
plato-republic-1.md:1: argue/answered: S4: answered no, which keeps plato-republic-1.logic.r1, to: Suppose "he blames injustice" is true and "owing to cowardice or age or some weakness, he has not the power of being unjust" is false. Is S4, "He only blames injustice who, owing to cowardice or age or some weakness, has not the power of being unjust.", true then? [in 1 of 2 runs]
plato-republic-1.md:1: argue/readings: S5 is read 2 ways: plato-republic-1.logic.r1 and plato-republic-1.logic.r1.2 have "gains_power → becomes_unjust"; plato-republic-1.logic.r1.3 has "blames_injustice ∧ gains_power → becomes_unjust"; they differ when only "he obtains the power of being unjust" is true, which the second allows
plato-republic-1.md:1: argue/answered: S5: answered no, which keeps plato-republic-1.logic.r1 and plato-republic-1.logic.r1.2, to: Suppose "he obtains the power of being unjust" is true and "he blames injustice" and "he immediately becomes unjust as far as he can be" are false. Is S5, "And this is proved by the fact that when he obtains the power, he immediately becomes unjust as far as he can be.", true then? [in 1 of 2 runs]
plato-republic-1.logic.r1: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: plato-republic-1.logic.r1 and plato-republic-1.logic.r1.3 have "no_principle → ¬superior_honours"; plato-republic-1.logic.r1.2 has "fares_well → no_principle" and "no_principle → ¬superior_honours"; they differ when only "if we unite the worst injustice with a deceitful regard to appearances, we shall fare to our mind with gods and men, in life and after death" is true, which the first allows
plato-republic-1.md:1: argue/negation: plato-republic-1.logic.r1 C (S2): the sentence states "a man who has any superiority of mind or person or rank or wealth is willing to honour justice", with no word of denial, but C denies it: it reads "it is not so that a man who has any superiority of mind or person or rank or wealth is willing to honour justice"
